🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗛𝗣𝗔 — 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗜𝘀 𝗬𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗦𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes application is experiencing high traffic, but the number of Pods is not increasing even though an HPA is configured. How will you troubleshoot it?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately increase the replica count manually. First, I'd investigate why the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) isn't scaling.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check HPA status

kubectl get hpa -n <namespace>
kubectl describe hpa <hpa-name> -n <namespace>

Look for current metrics, target values, events and scaling conditions.

2️⃣ Check resource metrics

kubectl top pods -n <namespace>

If metrics are unavailable, check whether Metrics Server is installed and working.

3️⃣ Verify CPU requests

HPA CPU utilization is calculated relative to CPU requests. If CPU requests are missing, CPU-based utilization may be unavailable.

kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace>

4️⃣ Check minimum and maximum replicas

Review the HPA configuration for "minReplicas", "maxReplicas" and target utilization.

The HPA cannot scale beyond its configured maximum.

5️⃣ Check Deployment health

kubectl get deployment -n <namespace>
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

Even when HPA requests more replicas, insufficient cluster capacity or scheduling problems can leave new Pods Pending.

6️⃣ Check scaling behavior

Investigate whether the workload's configured metrics, scaling policies or stabilization windows are affecting scaling decisions.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

HPA configured ≠ automatic scaling guaranteed.

Always check metrics, resource requests, replica limits, HPA events and cluster capacity before changing production settings.
